※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A colon with something after it on the same line opens a block that the end of
※ that line closes.  It is the layout notation with the indentation left out and
※ not a notation of its own: the same statements, the same separators, the same
※ everything -- so `if b: 0u6 else: 1u6` is what it looks like, and so is a
※ function whose whole body is one line.
※
※ **What closes it** is the end of the line, or anything on that line that
※ cannot continue it: the `else` or `elif` of the same chain, or the brace of a
※ block it stands in.  A semicolon can continue it, so two statements may stand
※ there the way two may stand on any line.
※
※ **A block written this way may not open another** (3044).  The inner one would
※ end where the outer one does, so an `else` after the two would belong to
※ either and a reader would have to know a rule to say which.  C, Java and the
※ rest settle that by binding to the nearest `if`; here it is refused, and the
※ inner one is written in braces or the outer one is written out.
※
※ Compare: **Python**, whose `if b: x` this is, and which refuses the nested
※ case for the same reason; **C** and its family, where braces are optional and
※ the dangling `else` is settled by a rule; **Haskell**, whose layout rule has
※ the same notion of a block opened without a newline.

let counted: mut u8 = 0u8

※ A whole function on one line, which is what most of these are.
fn twice(a: u8) → u8: a + a

@[impure]
fn note(a: u8): counted ← a

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let b: bool = true
    ※ an arm and its else, both on the line
    let picked: u8 = if b: 1u8 else: 2u8
    ※ a statement, with the end of the line closing the block
    let n: mut u8 = 0u8
    if b: n ← 5u8
    ※ a whole chain on one line, each arm closed by the word that begins the
    ※ next: an `elif` and an `else` can continue no statement, so there is
    ※ nothing else they could be
    let graded: u8 = if n = 4u8: 1u8 elif n = 5u8: 2u8 else: 3u8
    ※ a loop
    let counter: mut u8 = 0u8
    while counter < 3u8: counter ← counter + 1u8
    ※ two statements a semicolon separates, which a line takes anywhere
    if b: note(7u8) ; n ← n + 1u8
    ※ a block written in braces inside one written on a line, which says
    ※ outright what the inner block is and so may nest
    if b: if n = 6u8 { n ← n + 10u8 } else { n ← 0u8 }
    ※ and the other way round, a line inside braces
    if b { if n = 16u8: n ← n + 1u8 }
    if twice(3u8) ≠ 6u8:
        1u6
    elif picked ≠ 1u8:
        2u6
    elif counted ≠ 7u8:
        3u6
    elif graded ≠ 2u8:
        4u6
    elif counter ≠ 3u8:
        5u6
    elif n ≠ 17u8:
        6u6
    else:
        0u6
